# urjtag: auto-discover the shape of the TAP with no part declaration at all.
#   jtag -n -q host/urjtag-discovery.jtag
#
# 'discovery' probes the IR length, then the DR length behind every possible
# instruction.  For this design it should report:
#
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0000 ...  9   (EXTEST -> boundary)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0001 ...  9   (SAMPLE -> boundary)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0010 ... 32   (IDCODE)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0011 ... 32   (USER)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0100 ...  9   (INTEST -> boundary)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0101 ... 71   (CONFIG -> CLB bitstream)
#   Detecting DR length for IR 0110..1111 ... 1   (fall through to BYPASS)
#
# That single command maps the whole instruction set - including the width of
# the CLB's configuration chain - without needing any BSDL or database entry.
# It takes a minute or so.
#
# Expect the IR-length step itself to fail:
#
#   Detecting IR length ... USB read failed (timeout expired ?)
#
# That is a probe limitation, not a fault in the design.  DirtyJTAG's CMD_XFER
# caps a single transfer at 62 bytes (496 bits) and urjtag's IR-length probe
# shifts a longer run than that.  The DR sweep below it is unaffected, and
# 'detect' reports the IR length correctly anyway.
cable DirtyJTAG
frequency 1000000
discovery
quit
